{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The Royal Society of London for Improving Natural Knowledge, commonly referred to as the Royal Society, is a learned society and the United Kingdom's national academy of sciences.", "subject": "The Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Founded on 28 November 1660, it was granted a royal charter by King Charles II and is the oldest continuously existing scientific academy in the world.", "subject": "King Charles II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The society is governed by its Council, which is chaired by the society's president, according to a set of statutes and standing orders.", "subject": "Council,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The members of Council and the president are elected from and by its Fellows, the basic members of the society, who are themselves elected by existing Fellows.", "subject": "Council", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "As of 2020, there are about 1,700 fellows, allowed to use the postnominal title FRS (Fellow of the Royal Society), with up to 73 new fellows appointed each year from a pool of around 800 candidates.", "subject": "Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "There are also royal fellows, honorary fellows and foreign members.", "subject": "There", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Up to 24 new foreign members are appointed each year (from the same pool of 800) and they are allowed to use the postnominal title ForMemRS (Foreign Member of the Royal Society).", "subject": "Foreign Member", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The Royal Society president is Paul Nurse, who took over from Sir Adrian Smith in December 2025.", "subject": "The Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Since 1967, the society has been based at 6–9 Carlton House Terrace, a Grade I listed building in central London which was previously used by the Embassy of Germany, London.", "subject": "Carlton House Terrace", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Founding and early years ===\n\nThe Invisible College has been described as a precursor group to the Royal Society of London, consisting of a number of natural philosophers around Robert Boyle.", "subject": "The Invisible College", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The concept of \"invisible college\" is mentioned in German Rosicrucian pamphlets in the early 17th century.", "subject": "German Rosicrucian", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Ben Jonson in England referenced the idea, related in meaning to Francis Bacon's House of Solomon, in a masque The Fortunate Isles and Their Union from 1624/5.", "subject": "Ben Jonson", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The term accrued currency in the exchanges of correspondence within the Republic of Letters.", "subject": "Republic", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "In letters dated 1646 and 1647, Boyle refers to \"our invisible college\" or \"our philosophical college\".", "subject": "Boyle", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Three dated letters are the basic documentary evidence: Boyle sent them to Isaac Marcombes (Boyle's former tutor and a Huguenot, who was then in Geneva), Francis Tallents who at that point was a fellow of Magdalene College, Cambridge, and London-based Samuel Hartlib.", "subject": "Isaac Marcombes", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "The Royal Society started from groups of physicians and natural philosophers, meeting at a variety of locations, including Gresham College in London and Wadham College in Oxford University.", "subject": "The Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "They were influenced by the \"new science\", as promoted by Francis Bacon in his New Atlantis, from approximately 1645 onwards.", "subject": "Francis Bacon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "A group known as \"Philosophical Society of Oxford\" was run by John Wilkins, Warden of Wadham College under a set of rules still retained by the Bodleian Library.", "subject": "Philosophical Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "After the English Restoration, there were regular meetings at Gresham College.", "subject": "English Restoration", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "It is widely held that these groups were the inspiration for the foundation of the Royal Society.", "subject": "Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "An alternative view of the founding, held at the time, was that it was due to the influence of French scientists and the Montmor Academy in 1657, reports of which were sent back to England by English scientists attending.", "subject": "Montmor Academy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "This view was held by Jean-Baptiste du Hamel, Giovanni Domenico Cassini, Bernard le Bovier de Fontenelle and Melchisédech Thévenot at the time and has some grounding in that Henry Oldenburg, the society's first secretary, had attended the Montmor Academy meeting.", "subject": "Giovanni Domenico Cassini", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "Robert Hooke, however, disputed this, writing that:\n\n[Cassini] makes, then, Mr Oldenburg to have been the instrument, who inspired the English with a desire to imitate the French, in having Philosophical Clubs, or Meetings; and that this was the occasion of founding the Royal Society, and making the French the first.", "subject": "Robert Hooke", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "I will not say, that Mr Oldenburg did rather inspire the French to follow the English, or, at least, did help them, and hinder us.", "subject": "Mr Oldenburg", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Royal Society", "statement": "But 'tis well known who were the principal men that began and promoted that design, both in this city and in Oxford; and that a long while before Mr Oldenburg came into England.", "subject": "Mr Oldenburg", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Paul Rudolf Carnap (; German: [ˈkaʁnaːp]; 18 May 1891 – 14 September 1970) was a German philosopher and logician who was active in Europe before 1935 and in the United States thereafter.", "subject": "Paul Rudolf Carnap", "pid": "P569", "object": "18 May 1891"}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He was a major member of the Vienna Circle and an advocate of logical positivism.", "subject": "Vienna Circle", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "== Early life and education ==\n\nCarnap was born Paul Rudolf Carnap on 18 May 1891 in Ronsdorf, in present-day Wuppertal, Germany.", "subject": "Paul Rudolf Carnap", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Carnap's father rose from being a poor ribbon-weaver to be the owner of a ribbon-making factory.", "subject": "Carnap's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "His mother came from an academic family; her father was an educational reformer and her oldest brother was the archaeologist Wilhelm Dörpfeld.", "subject": "Wilhelm Dörpfeld", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "As a ten-year-old, Carnap accompanied Dörpfeld on an expedition to Greece.", "subject": "Carnap", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "At 14, Carnap taught himself Esperanto, and he attended the World Congress of Esperanto in Dresden in 1908.", "subject": "World Congress", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He would later attend the 1924 Congress in Vienna, where he met his fellow Esperantist Otto Neurath for the first time.", "subject": "Esperantist Otto Neurath", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Carnap was raised in a profoundly religious Protestant family, but later became an atheist.", "subject": "Carnap", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He began his formal education at the Barmen Gymnasium and the Carolo-Alexandrinum Gymnasium in Jena.", "subject": "Barmen Gymnasium", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "From 1910 to 1914, he attended the University of Jena, intending to write a thesis in physics.", "subject": "From", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He also intently studied Immanuel Kant's Critique of Pure Reason during a course taught by Bruno Bauch, and was one of the very few students to attend Gottlob Frege's courses in mathematical logic.", "subject": "Immanuel Kant", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "During his university years, he became enthralled with the German Youth Movement.", "subject": "German Youth Movement", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "While Carnap held moral and political opposition to World War I, he served in the German army.", "subject": "While Carnap", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "After three years of service, he was given permission to study physics at the University of Berlin, 1917–18, where Albert Einstein was a newly appointed professor.", "subject": "Albert Einstein", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "In August 1918 Carnap joined the Independent Social Democratic Party and worked as a journalist for left-wing newspapers.", "subject": "In August", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He then attended the University of Jena, where he wrote a thesis defining an axiomatic theory of space and time.", "subject": "University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "The physics department said it was too philosophical, and Bruno Bauch of the philosophy department said it was pure physics.", "subject": "Bruno Bauch", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Carnap then wrote another thesis in 1921, under Bauch's supervision, on the theory of space in a more orthodox Kantian style, published as Der Raum (Space) in a supplemental issue of Kant-Studien (1922).", "subject": "Der Raum", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Frege's course exposed him to Bertrand Russell's work on logic and philosophy, which gave a sense of direction to his studies.", "subject": "Bertrand Russell", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "He wrote a letter to Russell, who responded by copying by hand long passages from his Principia Mathematica for Carnap's benefit, as neither Carnap nor his university could afford a copy of this epochal work.", "subject": "Principia Mathematica", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "In 1924 and 1925, he attended seminars led by Edmund Husserl, the founder of phenomenology, and continued to write on physics from a logical positivist perspective.", "subject": "Edmund Husserl", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "== Career ==\nCarnap discovered a kindred spirit when he met Hans Reichenbach at a 1923 conference.", "subject": "Hans Reichenbach", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Reichenbach introduced Carnap to Moritz Schlick, a professor at the University of Vienna who offered Carnap a position in his department, which Carnap accepted in 1926.", "subject": "Moritz Schlick", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Rudolf Carnap", "statement": "Carnap thereupon joined an informal group of Viennese intellectuals that came to be known as the Vienna Circle, directed largely by Schlick and including Hans Hahn, Friedrich Waismann, Otto Neurath, and Herbert Feigl, with occasional visits by Hahn's student Kurt Gödel.", "subject": "Vienna Circle", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Developed from ancient and medieval practices, it acknowledges that cognitive assumptions can distort the interpretation of the observation.", "subject": "Developed", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Scientific inquiry includes creating a testable hypothesis through inductive reasoning, testing it through experiments and statistical analysis, and adjusting or discarding the hypothesis based on the results.", "subject": "Scientific", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Although procedures vary across fields, the underlying process is often similar.", "subject": "Although", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Hypotheses can be very specific or broad but must be falsifiable, implying that it is possible to identify a possible outcome of an experiment or observation that conflicts with predictions deduced from the hypothesis; otherwise, the hypothesis cannot be meaningfully tested.", "subject": "Hypotheses", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "While the scientific method is often presented as a fixed sequence of steps, it actually represents a set of general principles.", "subject": "While", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Not all steps take place in every scientific inquiry (nor to the same degree), and they are not always in the same order.", "subject": "Not", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Numerous discoveries have not followed the textbook model of the scientific method, and, in some cases, chance has played a role.", "subject": "Numerous", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "== History ==\n\nThe history of the scientific method is different from the history of science itself.", "subject": "History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Different early expressions of empiricism and the scientific method can be found throughout history, for instance with the ancient Stoics, Aristotle, Epicurus, Alhazen, Avicenna, Al-Biruni, Roger Bacon, and William of Ockham.", "subject": "Roger Bacon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "In the Scientific Revolution of the 16th and 17th centuries, some of the most important developments were the furthering of empiricism by Francis Bacon and Robert Hooke, the rationalist approach described by René Descartes, and inductivism, which was further brought to particular prominence by scientists such as Isaac Newton and those who followed him.", "subject": "Scientific Revolution", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Newton postulated four principles which form the basis of modern science, and refined the scientific method.", "subject": "Newton", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Experiments were advocated by Francis Bacon and performed by Giambattista della Porta, Johannes Kepler, and Galileo Galilei.", "subject": "Francis Bacon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "There was particular development aided by theoretical works by the skeptic Francisco Sanches, by idealists as well as empiricists John Locke, George Berkeley, and David Hume.", "subject": "Francisco Sanches", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Peirce formulated the hypothetico-deductive model in the 20th century, and the model has undergone significant revision since.", "subject": "Peirce", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Throughout the 1830s and 1850s, when Baconianism was popular, naturalists like William Whewell, John Herschel, and John Stuart Mill engaged in debates over \"induction\" and \"facts,\" and were focused on how to generate knowledge.", "subject": "William Whewell", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "=== Modern use and critical thought ===\nThe term scientific method came into popular use in the twentieth century; Dewey's 1910 book, How We Think, inspired popular guidelines.", "subject": "How We Think", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Although there was growth through the middle of the twentieth century, by the 1960s and 1970s numerous influential philosophers of science such as Thomas Kuhn and Paul Feyerabend had questioned the universality of the \"scientific method\", and largely replaced the notion of science as a homogeneous and universal method with that of it being a heterogeneous and local practice.", "subject": "Thomas Kuhn", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "In particular, Paul Feyerabend, in the 1975 first edition of his book Against Method, argued against there being any universal rules of science; Karl Popper, and Gauch 2003, disagreed with Feyerabend's claim.", "subject": "Paul Feyerabend", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Later stances include physicist Lee Smolin's 2013 essay \"There Is No Scientific Method\", in which he espouses two ethical principles, and historian of science Daniel Thurs' chapter in the 2015 book Newton's Apple and Other Myths about Science, which concluded that the scientific method is a myth or, at best, an idealization.", "subject": "Lee Smolin", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "As myths are beliefs, they are subject to the narrative fallacy, as pointed out by Taleb.", "subject": "Taleb.", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Philosophers Robert Nola and Howard Sankey, in their 2007 book Theories of Scientific Method, said that debates over the scientific method continue, and argued that Feyerabend, despite the title of Against Method, accepted certain rules of method and attempted to justify those rules with a meta methodology.", "subject": "Philosophers Robert Nola", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Staddon (2017) argues it is a mistake to try following rules in the absence of an algorithmic scientific method; in that case, \"science is best understood through examples\".", "subject": "Staddon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "But algorithmic methods, such as disproof of existing theory by experiment have been used since Alhacen (1027) and his Book of Optics, and Galileo (1638) and his Two New Sciences, and The Assayer, which still stand as scientific method.", "subject": "Two New Sciences", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "== Elements of inquiry ==\n\n\n=== Overview ===\n\nThe scientific method is the process by which science is carried out.", "subject": "Elements", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Scientific method", "statement": "Historically, the development of the scientific method was critical to the Scientific Revolution.", "subject": "Scientific Revolution", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Set theory is the branch of mathematical logic that studies sets, which can be informally described as collections of objects.", "subject": "Set", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Although objects of any kind can be collected into a set, set theory – as a branch of mathematics – is mostly concerned with those that are relevant to mathematics as a whole.", "subject": "Although", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "The modern study of set theory was initiated by the German mathematicians Richard Dedekind and Georg Cantor in the 1870s.", "subject": "Richard Dedekind", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "In particular, Georg Cantor is commonly considered the founder of set theory.", "subject": "Georg Cantor", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "After the discovery of paradoxes within naive set theory (e.g.", "subject": "After", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Russell's paradox, Cantor's paradox, and the Burali-Forti paradox, et al.), various axiomatic systems were proposed in the early twentieth century, of which Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory (with or without the axiom of choice) is still the best-known and most studied.", "subject": "Russell's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Set theory is commonly employed as a foundational system for the whole of mathematics, particularly in the form of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice.", "subject": "Set", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Besides its foundational role, set theory also provides the framework to develop a mathematical theory of infinity, and has various applications in computer science (such as in the theory of relational algebra), philosophy, formal semantics, and evolutionary dynamics.", "subject": "Besides", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Its foundational appeal, together with its paradoxes, and its implications for the concept of infinity and its multiple applications have made set theory an area of major interest for logicians and philosophers of mathematics.", "subject": "Its", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Contemporary research into set theory covers a vast array of topics, ranging from the structure of the real number line to the study of the consistency of large cardinals.", "subject": "Contemporary", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Early history ===\n\nThe basic notion of grouping objects has existed since at least the emergence of numbers, and the notion of treating sets as their own objects has existed since at least the Tree of Porphyry in 3rd-century CE.", "subject": "History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "The simplicity and ubiquity of sets makes it hard to determine the origin of sets as now used in mathematics; however, Bernard Bolzano's Paradoxes of the Infinite (Paradoxien des Unendlichen, 1851) is generally considered the first rigorous introduction of sets to mathematics.", "subject": "Bernard Bolzano", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "In his work, he (among other things) expanded on Galileo's paradox, and introduced one-to-one correspondence of infinite sets, for example between the intervals \n  \n    \n      \n        [\n        0\n        ,\n        5\n        ]\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle [0,5]}\n  \n and \n  \n    \n      \n        [\n        0\n        ,\n        12\n        ]\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle [0,12]}\n  \n by the relation \n  \n    \n      \n        5\n        y\n        =\n        12\n        x\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle 5y=12x}\n  \n.", "subject": "Galileo's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "However, he resisted saying these sets were equinumerous, and his work is generally considered to have been uninfluential in mathematics of his time.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Before mathematical set theory, basic concepts of infinity were considered to be in the domain of philosophy (see: Infinity (philosophy) and Infinity § History).", "subject": "Before", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Since the 5th century BC, beginning with Greek philosopher Zeno of Elea in the West (and early Indian mathematicians in the East), mathematicians had struggled with the concept of infinity.", "subject": "Since", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "With the development of calculus in the late 17th century, philosophers began to generally distinguish between potential and actual infinity, wherein mathematics was only considered in the latter.", "subject": "With", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Carl Friedrich Gauss famously stated: Infinity is nothing more than a figure of speech which helps us talk about limits.", "subject": "Carl Friedrich Gauss", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Bernhard Riemann's lecture On the Hypotheses which lie at the Foundations of Geometry (1854) proposed new ideas about topology.", "subject": "Bernhard Riemann", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "His lectures also introduced the concept of basing mathematics in terms of sets or manifolds in the sense of a class (which he called Mannigfaltigkeit) now called point-set topology.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "The lecture was published by Richard Dedekind in 1868, along with Riemann's paper on trigonometric series (which presented the Riemann integral).", "subject": "Richard Dedekind", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "A young Georg Cantor entered into this area, which led him to the study of point-sets.", "subject": "Georg Cantor", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Around 1871, influenced by Riemann, Dedekind began working with sets in his publications, which dealt very clearly and precisely with equivalence relations, partitions of sets, and homomorphisms.", "subject": "Around", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "Thus, many of the usual set-theoretic procedures of twentieth-century mathematics go back to his work.", "subject": "Thus,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Set theory", "statement": "However, he did not publish a formal explanation of his set theory until 1888.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Social epistemology refers to a broad set of approaches that can be taken in epistemology (the study of knowledge) that construes human knowledge as a collective achievement.", "subject": "Social", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Another way of characterizing social epistemology is as the evaluation of the social dimensions of knowledge or information.", "subject": "Another", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Social epistemology also examines the social justification of belief.", "subject": "Social", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "One of the enduring difficulties with defining \"social epistemology\" that arises is the attempt to determine what the word \"knowledge\" means in this context.", "subject": "One", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "There is also a challenge in arriving at a definition of \"social\" which satisfies academics from different disciplines.", "subject": "There", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Social epistemologists may exist working in many of the disciplines of the humanities and social sciences, most commonly in philosophy and sociology.", "subject": "Social", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "== History of the term ==\nThe consideration of social dimensions of knowledge in relation to philosophy started in 380 B.C.E with Plato’s dialogue: Charmides.", "subject": "History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "This dialogue included Socrates' argument about whether anyone is capable of examining if another man's claim that he knows something, is true or not.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Charmides also explored the tendency of the utopian vision of social relations to degenerate into dystopian fantasy.", "subject": "Charmides", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "In 1936, Karl Mannheim turned Karl Marx‘s theory of ideology (which interpreted the “social” aspect in epistemology to be of a political or sociological nature) into an analysis of how the human society develops and functions in this respect.", "subject": "Karl Mannheim", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Particularly, this Marxist analysis prompted Mannheim to write Ideology and Utopia, which investigated the classical sociology of knowledge and the construct of ideology.", "subject": "Particularly,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "The term “social epistemology” was first coined by the library scientists Margaret Egan.", "subject": "Margaret Egan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "and Jesse Shera in a Library Quarterly paper at the University of Chicago Graduate Library School in the 1950s.", "subject": "Jesse Shera", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "The term was used by Robert K.", "subject": "Robert", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Merton in a 1972 article in the American Journal of Sociology and then by Steven Shapin in 1979.", "subject": "American Journal", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "However, it was not until the 1980s that the current sense of “social epistemology” began to emerge.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "In 1987, the philosophical journal Synthese published a special issue on social epistemology which included two authors that have since taken the branch of epistemology in two divergent directions: Alvin Goldman and Steve Fuller.", "subject": "Alvin Goldman", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Fuller founded a journal called Social Epistemology: A journal of knowledge, culture, and policy in 1987 and published his first book, Social Epistemology, in 1988.", "subject": "Social Epistemology", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Goldman’s Knowledge in a Social World came out in 1999.", "subject": "Social World", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Goldman advocates for a type of epistemology which is sometimes called “veritistic epistemology” because of its large emphasis on truth.", "subject": "Goldman", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "This type of epistemology is sometimes seen to side with “essentialism” as opposed to “multiculturalism”.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "But Goldman has argued that this association between veritistic epistemology and essentialism is not necessary.", "subject": "But Goldman", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "He describes Social Epistemology as knowledge derived from one’s interactions with another person, group or society.", "subject": "Social Epistemology", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "Goldman looks into one of the two strategies of the socialization of epistemology.", "subject": "Goldman", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Social epistemology", "statement": "This strategy includes the evaluation of social factors that impact knowledge formed on true belief.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Marie-Sophie Germain (French: [maʁi sɔfi ʒɛʁmɛ̃]; 1 April 1776 – 27 June 1831) was a French mathematician, physicist, and philosopher.", "subject": "Sophie Germain", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Despite initial opposition from her parents and difficulties presented by society, she gained education from books in her father's library, including ones by Euler, and from correspondence under the pseudonym of Monsieur Le Blanc with famous mathematicians, such as Lagrange, Legendre, and Gauss.", "subject": "Monsieur Le Blanc", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "One of the pioneers of elasticity theory, she won the grand prize from the Paris Academy of Sciences for her essay on the subject.", "subject": "Paris Academy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Her work on Fermat's Last Theorem provided a foundation for mathematicians exploring the subject for hundreds of years after.", "subject": "Last Theorem", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Because of prejudice against her sex, she was unable to make a career out of mathematics, but she worked independently throughout her life.", "subject": "Because", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Before her death, Gauss had recommended that she be awarded an honorary degree, but that never occurred.", "subject": "Before", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "On 27 June 1831, she died from breast cancer.", "subject": "June", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Following the publication of a biography in the late 1870s marking the centenary of her life, a street and a girls' school were then named after her.", "subject": "Following", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "The Academy of Sciences established the Sophie Germain Prize in her honour.", "subject": "The Academy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "== Early life ==\n\n\n=== Family ===\nMarie-Sophie Germain was born in a house on Rue Saint-Denis on 1 April 1776, in Paris, France, to Marie-Madeline (née Gruguelin) and Ambroise-François Germain and was baptised the same day.", "subject": "Sophie Germain", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "She was from a bourgeois family.", "subject": "She", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "According to most sources, her father was a wealthy silk merchant, though some believe he was a goldsmith.", "subject": "According", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "In 1789, he was elected as a representative of the bourgeoisie to the États-Généraux, which he saw change into the National Assembly.", "subject": "National Assembly", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "It has been assumed that Sophie witnessed many discussions between her father and his friends on politics and philosophy.", "subject": "Sophie", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Gray proposes that after his political career, Ambroise-François became the director of a bank.", "subject": "Gray", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "The family remained well-off enough to support Germain throughout her adult life.", "subject": "Germain", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Marie-Sophie had one older sister, Marie-Madeline and one younger sister, Angélique-Ambroise.", "subject": "Marie-Sophie", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Her mother was also named Marie-Madeline, and this plethora of \"Maries\" may have been the reason she went by Sophie.", "subject": "Her", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Germain's nephew Armand-Jacques Lherbette, her sister Marie-Madeline's son, published some of Germain's work after she died (see Work in Philosophy).", "subject": "Jacques Lherbette", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "=== Introduction to mathematics ===\nWhen Germain was 13, the Bastille fell, and the revolutionary atmosphere of the city forced her to stay inside.", "subject": "When Germain", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "For entertainment, she turned to her father's library.", "subject": "For", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Here she found Jean-Étienne Montucla's L'Histoire des Mathématiques, and his story of the death of Archimedes intrigued her.", "subject": "Here", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "Germain thought that if the geometry method, which at that time referred to all of pure mathematics, could hold such fascination for Archimedes, it was a subject worthy of study.", "subject": "Germain", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "So she pored over every book on mathematics in her father's library, even teaching herself Latin and Greek, so she could read works like those of Isaac Newton and Leonhard Euler.", "subject": "Isaac Newton", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Sophie Germain", "statement": "She also enjoyed Traité d'Arithmétique by Étienne Bézout and Le Calcul Différentiel by Jacques Antoine-Joseph Cousin.", "subject": "Le Calcul Différentiel", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Originally founded in 1842 in Berlin, it expanded internationally in the 1960s, and through mergers in the 1990s and a sale to venture capitalists it fused with Wolters Kluwer and eventually became part of Springer Nature in 2015.", "subject": "Originally", "pid": "P571", "object": "1842"}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Springer Science+Business Media, commonly known as Springer, is a German multinational publishing company of books, e-books and peer-reviewed journals in science, humanities, technical and medical (STM) publishing.", "subject": "Springer Science", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "It is owned by Springer Nature.", "subject": "Springer Nature", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Springer has major offices in Berlin, Heidelberg, Dordrecht, and New York City.", "subject": "New York City", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "== History ==\n\nJulius Springer founded Springer-Verlag in Berlin in 1842 and his son Ferdinand Springer grew it from a small firm of 4 employees into Germany's then second-largest academic publisher with 65 staff in 1872.", "subject": "Julius Springer", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "The company's canting logo is a chess knight; Springer (literally \"jumper\") is the word for the piece in German.", "subject": "Springer", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 1964, Springer expanded its business internationally, opening an office in New York City.", "subject": "New York City", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Offices in Tokyo, Paris, Milan, Hong Kong, and Delhi soon followed.", "subject": "Hong Kong", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 1999, the academic publishing company BertelsmannSpringer was formed after the media and entertainment company Bertelsmann bought a majority stake in Springer-Verlag.", "subject": "BertelsmannSpringer", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2003, the British investment groups Cinven and Candover bought BertelsmannSpringer from Bertelsmann.", "subject": "British", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "They merged the company in 2004 with the Dutch publisher Kluwer Academic Publishers (successor of D.", "subject": "Kluwer Academic Publishers", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Junk, Plenum Publishers, most of Chapman & Hall, and Baltzer Science Publishers) which they bought from Wolters Kluwer in 2002, to form Springer Science+Business Media.", "subject": "Plenum Publishers", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2006, Springer acquired Humana Press.", "subject": "Humana Press", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Springer acquired the open-access publisher BioMed Central in October 2008 for an undisclosed amount.", "subject": "Springer", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2009, Cinven and Candover sold Springer to two private equity firms, EQT AB and Government of Singapore Investment Corporation, confirmed in February 2010 after the competition authorities in the US and in Europe approved the transfer.", "subject": "Singapore Investment Corporation", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2011, Springer acquired Pharma Marketing and Publishing Services (MPS) from Wolters Kluwer.", "subject": "Pharma Marketing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2013, the London-based private equity firm BC Partners acquired a majority stake in Springer from EQT and GIC for $4.4 billion.", "subject": "London-based", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In January 2015, Holtzbrinck Publishing Group / Nature Publishing Group and Springer Science+Business Media announced a merger.", "subject": "In January", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "in May 2015 they concluded the transaction and formed a new joint venture company, Springer Nature with Holtzbrinck in the majority 53% share and BC Partners retaining 47% interest in the company.", "subject": "Springer Nature", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "== Products ==\nIn 1996, Springer launched electronic book and journal content on its SpringerLink site.", "subject": "Products", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "SpringerImages was launched in 2008.", "subject": "SpringerImages", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "In 2009, SpringerMaterials, a platform for accessing the Landolt-Börnstein database of research and information on materials and their properties, was launched.", "subject": "SpringerMaterials,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "AuthorMapper is a free online tool for visualizing scientific research that enables document discovery based on author locations and geographic maps, helping users explore patterns in scientific research, identify literature trends, discover collaborative relationships, and locate experts in several scientific/medical fields.", "subject": "AuthorMapper", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Springer Protocols contained a collection of laboratory protocols, recipes that provide step-by-step instructions for conducting experiments, which in 2018 was made available in SpringerLink instead.", "subject": "Springer Protocols", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Springer Science+Business Media", "statement": "Book publications include major reference works, textbooks, monographs and book series; more than 168,000 titles are available as e-books in 24 subject collections.", "subject": "Book", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Srinivasa Ramanujan Iyengar (22 December 1887 – 26 April 1920) was an Indian mathematician who worked during the early 20th century.", "subject": "Srinivasa Ramanujan Iyengar", "pid": "P569", "object": "22 December 1887"}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Ramanujan initially developed his own mathematical research in isolation.", "subject": "Ramanujan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "According to Hans Eysenck, \"he tried to interest the leading professional mathematicians in his work, but failed for the most part.", "subject": "Hans Eysenck", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "What he had to show them was too novel, too unfamiliar, and additionally presented in unusual ways; they could not be bothered.\" Seeking mathematicians who could better understand his work, in 1913 he began a mail correspondence with the English mathematician G.", "subject": "What", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Hardy at the University of Cambridge.", "subject": "Hardy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Recognising Ramanujan's work as extraordinary, Hardy arranged for him to travel to Cambridge.", "subject": "Recognising Ramanujan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "In his notes, Hardy commented that Ramanujan had produced groundbreaking new theorems, including some that \"defeated me completely; I had never seen anything in the least like them before\", and some recently proven but highly advanced results.", "subject": "Hardy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Throughout his life, Ramanujan independently compiled nearly 3,900 results (mostly identities and equations).", "subject": "Throughout", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "Many were completely novel; his original and highly unconventional results, such as the Ramanujan prime, the Ramanujan theta function, partition formulae and mock theta functions, have opened entire new areas of work and inspired further research.", "subject": "Many", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "The Ramanujan Journal, a scientific journal, was established to publish work in all areas of mathematics influenced by Ramanujan, and his notebooks—containing summaries of his published and unpublished results—have been analysed and studied for decades since his death as a source of new mathematical ideas.", "subject": "The Ramanujan Journal", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "He became one of the youngest Fellows of the Royal Society and only the second Indian member, and the first Indian to be elected a Fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge.", "subject": "Royal Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "In 1919, ill health—now believed to have been hepatic amoebiasis (a complication from episodes of dysentery many years previously)—compelled Ramanujan's return to India, where he died in 1920 at the age of 32.", "subject": "Ramanujan's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "His last letters to Hardy, written in January 1920, show that he was still continuing to produce new mathematical ideas and theorems.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "His \"lost notebook\", containing discoveries from the last year of his life, caused great excitement among mathematicians when it was rediscovered in 1976.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "== Early life ==\n\nRamanujan (literally, \"younger brother of Rama\", a Hindu deity) was born in British-ruled India on 22 December 1887 into a Tamil Brahmin Iyengar family in Erode, in modern Tamil Nadu.", "subject": "Tamil Brahmin Iyengar", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "His father, Kuppuswamy Srinivasa Iyengar, originally from Thanjavur district, worked as a clerk in a sari shop.", "subject": "Kuppuswamy Srinivasa Iyengar", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "His mother, Komalatammal, was a housewife and sang at a local temple.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "They lived in a small traditional home on Sarangapani Sannidhi Street in the town of Kumbakonam.", "subject": "Sarangapani Sannidhi Street", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "When Ramanujan was a year and a half old, his mother gave birth to a son, Sadagopan, who died less than three months later.", "subject": "When Ramanujan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "In December 1889, Ramanujan contracted smallpox, but recovered.", "subject": "In December", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "He moved with his mother to her parents' house in Kanchipuram, near Madras (modern Chennai).", "subject": "Kanchipuram,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "His mother gave birth to two more children, in 1891 and 1894, both of whom died before their first birthdays.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "On 1 October 1892, Ramanujan was enrolled at the local school.", "subject": "October", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "After his maternal grandfather lost his job as a court official in Kanchipuram, Ramanujan and his mother moved back to Kumbakonam, and he was enrolled in Kangayan Primary School.", "subject": "Kangayan Primary School", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Srinivasa Ramanujan", "statement": "When his paternal grandfather died, he was sent back to his maternal grandparents, then living in Madras.", "subject": "When", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (SEP) is a free online philosophy resource published and maintained by Stanford University, encompassing both an online encyclopedia of philosophy and peer-reviewed original publication.", "subject": "The Stanford Encyclopedia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Each entry is written and maintained by an expert in the field, including professors from many academic institutions worldwide.", "subject": "Each", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Authors contributing to the encyclopedia give Stanford University the permission to publish the articles, but retain the copyright to those articles.", "subject": "Stanford University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "== Approach and history ==\nAs of August 5, 2022, the SEP has 1,774 published entries.", "subject": "Approach", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Apart from its online status, the encyclopedia uses the traditional academic approach of most encyclopedias and academic journals to achieve quality by means of specialist authors selected by an editor or an editorial committee that is competent (although not necessarily considered specialists) in the field covered by the encyclopedia and peer review.", "subject": "Apart", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "The encyclopedia was created in 1995 by Edward N.", "subject": "Edward", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Zalta, with the explicit aim of providing a dynamic encyclopedia that is updated regularly, and so does not become dated in the manner of conventional print encyclopedias.", "subject": "Zalta,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Initially, the SEP was developed with U.S.", "subject": "Initially,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "public funding from the National Endowment for the Humanities and the National Science Foundation.", "subject": "National Endowment", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "These institutions contribute under a plan devised by the SEP in collaboration with the Scholarly Publishing and Academic Resources Coalition, the International Coalition of Library Consortia, and the Southeastern Library Network, with matching funding from the National Endowment for the Humanities.", "subject": "Scholarly Publishing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "The logo depicts the initials of SEP in the shape of The Thinker, an original copy of which is co-owned by Stanford and which features across the university's iconography and culture.", "subject": "The Thinker", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "== See also ==\nEncyclopedia of Philosophy\nInternet Encyclopedia of Philosophy\nNelson's Perpetual Loose Leaf Encyclopaedia\nRoutledge Encyclopedia of Philosophy\nList of online encyclopedias\n\n\n== References ==\n\n\n== External links ==\nOfficial website\nOfficial mirror websites:\nStanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy mirror from the Institute for Logic, Language and Computation of the University of Amsterdam\nStanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy mirror from University of Sydney Library", "subject": "Philosophy\nInternet Encyclopedia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "The charter for the encyclopedia allows for rival articles on a single topic to reflect reasoned disagreements among scholars.", "subject": "The", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "A long-term fundraising plan to preserve open access to the encyclopedia is supported by many university libraries and library consortia.", "subject": "Unknown", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy", "statement": "Approach and history ==", "subject": "Approach", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "=== Career ===\nAt age 14, Tao attended the Research Science Institute, a summer program for secondary students.", "subject": "Tao", "pid": "P69", "object": "Research Science Institute"}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Terence Chi-Shen Tao  (Chinese: 陶哲轩; pinyin: Táo Zhéxuān; born 17 July 1975) is an Australian and American mathematician who was awarded the Fields Medal in 2006 for his contributions to partial differential equations, combinatorics, harmonic analysis, and additive number theory.", "subject": "Terence Chi-Shen Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "He is a professor of mathematics at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), where he holds the James and Carol Collins Chair in the College of Letters and Sciences.", "subject": "Los Angeles", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Among his contributions to mathematics is the Green–Tao theorem on prime numbers, which he proved in 2004 in collaboration with Ben Green.", "subject": "Ben Green", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao was born to Chinese immigrant parents and raised in Adelaide, South Australia.", "subject": "South Australia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "After studying at Princeton and joining the faculty at UCLA, he went on to be a researcher, known for the diversity of his own interests and his collaborations with others.", "subject": "After", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao has won many prizes for his work, including the Fields Medal in 2006 and the Royal Medal and Breakthrough Prize in Mathematics in 2014.", "subject": "Fields Medal", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "He is a 2006 MacArthur Fellow.", "subject": "MacArthur", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao's published research includes topics in harmonic analysis, partial differential equations, algebraic combinatorics, arithmetic combinatorics, geometric combinatorics, probability theory, compressed sensing, and analytic number theory.", "subject": "Tao's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "== Early life and career ==\n\n\n=== Family ===\nTao's father, Billy Tao, was a Chinese paediatrician who was born in Shanghai and received a medical degree (MBBS) from the University of Hong Kong in 1969.", "subject": "Billy Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao's mother, Grace Leong, was born in Hong Kong; she received a first-class honours bachelor's degree majoring in mathematics and physics from the University of Hong Kong.", "subject": "Grace Leong", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "She was a secondary school teacher of mathematics and physics in Hong Kong.", "subject": "Hong Kong", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Billy and Grace met as students at the University of Hong Kong.", "subject": "Hong Kong", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "They then emigrated from Hong Kong to Australia in 1972.", "subject": "Hong Kong", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao also has two brothers, Trevor and Nigel, who are currently living in Australia.", "subject": "Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Both formerly represented Australia at the International Mathematical Olympiad.", "subject": "International Mathematical Olympiad", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "=== Childhood ===\nA child prodigy, Terence Tao skipped five grades.", "subject": "Terence Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao exhibited extraordinary mathematical abilities from an early age, attending university-level mathematics courses at the age of 9.", "subject": "Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "He is one of only three children in the history of the Johns Hopkins Study of Exceptional Talent program to have achieved a score of 700 or greater on the SAT math section while just eight years old; Tao scored a 760.", "subject": "Johns Hopkins Study", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Julian Stanley, Director of the Study of Mathematically Precocious Youth, stated that Tao had the greatest mathematical reasoning ability he had found in years of intensive searching.", "subject": "Julian Stanley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao was the youngest participant to date in the International Mathematical Olympiad, first competing at the age of ten; in 1986, 1987, and 1988, he won a bronze, silver, and gold medal, respectively.", "subject": "International Mathematical Olympiad", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "Tao remains the youngest winner of each of the three medals in the Olympiad's history.", "subject": "Tao", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "In 1991, he received his bachelor's and master's degrees at the age of 16 from Flinders University under the direction of Garth Gaudry.", "subject": "Flinders University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "In 1992, he won a postgraduate Fulbright Scholarship to undertake research in mathematics at Princeton University in the United States.", "subject": "Fulbright Scholarship", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Terence Tao", "statement": "From 1992 to 1996, Tao was a graduate student at Princeton University under the direction of Elias Stein, receiving his PhD at the age of 21.", "subject": "Princeton University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The New York Times (NYT) is a newspaper based in Manhattan, New York City.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The New York Times covers domestic, national, and international news, and publishes opinion pieces and reviews.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "One of the longest-running newspapers in the United States, the Times serves as one of the country's newspapers of record.", "subject": "United States", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "As of August 2025, The New York Times had 11.88 million total and 11.3 million online subscribers, both the highest numbers for any newspaper in the United States by a significant margin; the total also included 580,000 print subscribers.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The New York Times is published by the New York Times Company.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Since 1896, the company has been chaired by the Ochs-Sulzberger family.", "subject": "Since", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The Times is headquartered at The New York Times Building in Midtown Manhattan.", "subject": "The Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The Times was founded as the conservative New-York Daily Times in 1851, and came to national recognition in the 1870s with its aggressive coverage of corrupt politician Boss Tweed.", "subject": "The Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Following the Panic of 1893, Chattanooga Times publisher Adolph Ochs gained a controlling interest in the company.", "subject": "Chattanooga Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "In 1935, Ochs was succeeded by his son-in-law, Arthur Hays Sulzberger, who began a push into European news.", "subject": "Arthur Hays Sulzberger", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Sulzberger's son Arthur Ochs Sulzberger became publisher in 1963, adapting to a changing newspaper industry and introducing radical changes.", "subject": "Arthur Ochs Sulzberger", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "The New York Times was involved in the landmark 1964 U.S.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Supreme Court case New York Times Co.", "subject": "Supreme Court", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Sullivan, which restricted the ability of public officials to sue the media for defamation.", "subject": "Sullivan,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "In 1971, The New York Times published the Pentagon Papers, an internal Department of Defense document detailing the United States's historical involvement in the Vietnam War, despite pushback from then-president Richard Nixon.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "In the landmark decision New York Times Co.", "subject": "New York Times Co", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "United States (1971), the Supreme Court ruled that the First Amendment guaranteed the right to publish the Pentagon Papers.", "subject": "United States", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "In the 1980s, the Times began a two-decade progression to digital technology and launched nytimes.com in 1996.", "subject": "Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Currently, the Times maintains several regional bureaus staffed with journalists across six continents.", "subject": "Currently,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "It has expanded to several other publications, including The New York Times Magazine, The New York Times International Edition, and The New York Times Book Review.", "subject": "The New York Times Magazine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "In addition, the paper has produced several television series, podcasts—including The Daily—and games through The New York Times Games.", "subject": "The Daily", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "Among other accolades, it has been awarded the Pulitzer Prize 135 times since 1918, the most of any publication.", "subject": "Pulitzer Prize", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "However, The New York Times has been involved in a number of controversies in its history.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "According to a 2025 Pew Research Center study on educational differences among audiences of 30 major U.S.", "subject": "Pew Research Center", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "The New York Times", "statement": "news outlets, The New York Times had the highest proportion of college-educated readers among the daily newspapers surveyed, with 56% of its audience holding at least a bachelor's degree.", "subject": "The New York Times", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Thomas Samuel Kuhn (; July 18, 1922 – June 17, 1996) was an American historian and philosopher of science whose 1962 book The Structure of Scientific Revolutions was influential in both academic and popular circles.", "subject": "Thomas Samuel Kuhn", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "The book popularized the term paradigm shift, which has since become an English-language idiom.", "subject": "English-language", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Kuhn made several claims concerning the progress of scientific knowledge: that scientific fields undergo periodic \"paradigm shifts\" rather than solely progressing in a linear and continuous way, and that these paradigm shifts open up new approaches to understanding what scientists would never have considered valid before; and that the notion of scientific truth, at any given moment, cannot be established solely by objective criteria but is defined by a consensus of a scientific community.", "subject": "Kuhn", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Competing paradigms are frequently incommensurable; that is, there is no one-to-one correspondence of assumptions and terms.", "subject": "Competing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Thus, our comprehension of science can never rely wholly upon \"objectivity\" alone.", "subject": "Thus,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Science must account for subjective perspectives as well, since all objective conclusions are ultimately founded upon the subjective conditioning/worldview of its researchers and participants.", "subject": "Science", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "== Early life, family and education ==\nKuhn was born in Cincinnati, Ohio, in 1922 to Minette Stroock Kuhn and Samuel L.", "subject": "Minette Stroock Kuhn", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Kuhn, an industrial engineer, both Jewish though non-observant.", "subject": "Kuhn,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "The family moved to Manhattan when he was an infant.", "subject": "Manhattan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "From kindergarten through fifth grade, he was educated at Lincoln School, a private progressive school in Manhattan, which stressed independent thinking rather than learning facts and subjects.", "subject": "Lincoln School", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "The family then moved 40 mi (64 km) north to the small town of Croton-on-Hudson, New York, where, once again, he attended a private progressive school – Hessian Hills School.", "subject": "New York", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Here, in sixth through ninth grade, he learned to love mathematics.", "subject": "Here,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "He left Hessian Hills in 1937 and spent one year at the Solebury School before attending The Taft School in Watertown, Connecticut, graduating in 1940.", "subject": "Hessian Hills", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "He obtained his BSc degree in physics from Harvard College in 1943.", "subject": "Harvard College", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "As an undergraduate, he wrote for The Harvard Crimson and headed its editorial board.", "subject": "The Harvard Crimson", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "He also obtained MSc and PhD degrees in physics in 1946 and 1949, respectively, under the supervision of John Van Vleck, after a short period of World War II war work with Van Vleck at Harvard's secret Radio Research Laboratory that included travel to England, France, and Germany.", "subject": "John Van Vleck", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "== Career ==\nKuhn began his teaching career with a course in the history of science at Harvard from 1948 until 1957 as assistant professor of General Education and History of Science at the suggestion of university president James B.", "subject": "General Education", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "He was a Harvard Junior Fellow 1948–1951 and, as he states in the first pages of the preface to the second edition of The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, these three years of total academic freedom were crucial in allowing him to switch from studying physics to studying the history of science and philosophy of science.", "subject": "Harvard Junior Fellow", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "However, Conant's influence at Harvard declined rapidly over the course of the 50s and the general education program was refocused, and Kuhn was rejected for tenure in 1957.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Kuhn taught next, after Harvard, at the University of California, Berkeley, in both the philosophy department and the history department; he was named Professor of History of Science in 1961.", "subject": "Kuhn", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "At Berkeley, Kuhn served as director of the National Science Foundation project Sources for the History of Quantum Physics 1961–1964.", "subject": "At Berkeley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Kuhn interviewed and tape recorded Danish physicist Niels Bohr the day before Bohr's death.", "subject": "Niels Bohr", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "At Berkeley, he wrote and published (in 1962) his best known and most influential work: The Structure of Scientific Revolutions.", "subject": "At Berkeley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "In 1964, he joined Princeton University as the M.", "subject": "Princeton University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Thomas Kuhn", "statement": "Taylor Pyne Professor of Philosophy and History of Science.", "subject": "Taylor Pyne Professor", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Topology (from the Greek words τόπος, 'place, location', and λόγος, 'study') is the branch of mathematics concerned with the properties of a geometric object that are preserved under continuous deformations, such as stretching, twisting, crumpling, and bending; that is, without closing holes, opening holes, tearing, gluing, or passing through itself.", "subject": "Topology", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Euclidean spaces, and, more generally, metric spaces are examples of topological spaces, as any distance or metric defines a topology.", "subject": "Euclidean", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "The ideas underlying topology go back to Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, who in the 17th century envisioned the geometria situs and analysis situs.", "subject": "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Leonhard Euler's Seven Bridges of Königsberg problem and polyhedron formula are arguably the field's first theorems.", "subject": "Leonhard Euler", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "The term topology was introduced by Johann Benedict Listing in the 19th century, although, it was not until the first decades of the 20th century that the idea of a topological space was developed.", "subject": "Johann Benedict Listing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "== Motivation ==\n\nThe motivating insight behind topology is that some geometric problems depend not on the exact shape of the objects involved, but rather on the way they are put together.", "subject": "Motivation", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "For example, the square and the circle have many properties in common: they are both one-dimensional objects (from a topological point of view) and both separate the plane into two parts, the part inside and the part outside.", "subject": "For", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "In one of the first papers in topology, Leonhard Euler demonstrated that it was impossible to find a route through the town of Königsberg (now Kaliningrad) that would cross each of its seven bridges exactly once.", "subject": "Leonhard Euler", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "This result did not depend on the lengths of the bridges or on their distance from one another, but only on connectivity properties: which bridges connect to which islands or riverbanks.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "This Seven Bridges of Königsberg problem led to the branch of mathematics known as graph theory.", "subject": "This Seven Bridges", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Similarly, the hairy ball theorem of algebraic topology says that \"one cannot comb the hair flat on a hairy ball without creating a cowlick.\" This fact is immediately convincing to most people, even though they might not recognize the more formal statement of the theorem, that there is no nonvanishing continuous tangent vector field on the sphere.", "subject": "Similarly,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "As with the Bridges of Königsberg, the result does not depend on the shape of the sphere; it applies to any kind of smooth blob, as long as it has no holes.", "subject": "Bridges", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "From this need arises the notion of homeomorphism.", "subject": "From", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "The impossibility of crossing each bridge just once applies to any arrangement of bridges homeomorphic to those in Königsberg, and the hairy ball theorem applies to any space homeomorphic to a sphere.", "subject": "Königsberg,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Intuitively, two spaces are homeomorphic if one can be deformed into the other without cutting or gluing.", "subject": "Intuitively,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "A famous example, known as the \"Topologist's Breakfast\", is that a topologist cannot distinguish a coffee mug from a doughnut.", "subject": "Breakfast\",", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Homeomorphism can be considered the most basic topological equivalence.", "subject": "Homeomorphism", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Another is homotopy equivalence.", "subject": "Another", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "This is harder to describe without getting technical, but the essential notion is that two objects are homotopy equivalent if they both result from \"squishing\" some larger object.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "== History ==\n\nTopology, as a well-defined mathematical discipline, originates in the early part of the twentieth century, but some isolated results can be traced back several centuries.", "subject": "History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Among these are certain questions in geometry investigated by Leonhard Euler.", "subject": "Leonhard Euler", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "His 1736 paper on the Seven Bridges of Königsberg is regarded as one of the first practical applications of topology.", "subject": "Seven Bridges", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "On 14 November 1750, Euler wrote to a friend that he had realized the importance of the edges of a polyhedron.", "subject": "November", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "This led to his polyhedron formula, V − E + F = 2 (where V, E, and F respectively indicate the number of vertices, edges, and faces of the polyhedron).", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Topology", "statement": "Some authorities regard this analysis as the first theorem, signaling the birth of topology.", "subject": "Some", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity College is a constituent college of the University of Cambridge.", "subject": "Trinity College", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Founded in 1546 by King Henry VIII, Trinity is one of the largest Cambridge colleges, with the largest financial endowment of any college at Oxford or Cambridge.", "subject": "King Henry VIII", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity has some of the most distinctive architecture in Cambridge with its Great Court said to be the largest enclosed courtyard in Europe.", "subject": "Great Court", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Academically, Trinity performs exceptionally as measured by the Tompkins Table (the annual unofficial league table of Cambridge colleges), coming top from 2011 to 2017, and regaining the position in 2024.", "subject": "Tompkins Table", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Members of Trinity have been awarded 34 Nobel Prizes out of the 126 received by members of the University of Cambridge (more than any other Oxford or Cambridge college).", "subject": "Nobel Prizes", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Members of the college have received four Fields Medals, one Turing Award and one Abel Prize.", "subject": "Fields Medals", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity alumni include Francis Bacon, six British prime ministers (the highest number of any Cambridge college), physicists Isaac Newton, James Clerk Maxwell, Ernest Rutherford and Niels Bohr, mathematicians Srinivasa Ramanujan and Charles Babbage, poets Lord Byron and Lord Tennyson, English jurist Edward Coke, writers Vladimir Nabokov and A.", "subject": "Francis Bacon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Milne, historians Lord Macaulay and G.", "subject": "Lord Macaulay", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trevelyan, and philosophers Ludwig Wittgenstein and Bertrand Russell (whom the college expelled before reaccepting).", "subject": "Ludwig Wittgenstein", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Two members of the British royal family have studied at Trinity and been awarded degrees: Prince William of Gloucester and Edinburgh, who gained an MA degree in 1790, and King Charles III, who was awarded a lower second-class BA degree in 1970.", "subject": "Prince William", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity's many college societies include the Trinity Mathematical Society, the oldest mathematical university society in the United Kingdom, and the First and Third Trinity Boat Club, its rowing club, which gives its name to the May Ball.", "subject": "Trinity Mathematical Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Along with Christ's, Jesus, King's and St John's colleges, it has provided several well-known members of the Cambridge Apostles, an intellectual secret society.", "subject": "St John", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "In 1848, Trinity hosted a meeting at which Cambridge undergraduates representing fee-paying private schools codified a set of early rules of football known as the Cambridge rules.", "subject": "Trinity", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity's sister college is Christ Church, Oxford.", "subject": "Christ Church", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "Trinity has been linked with Westminster School since the school's re-foundation in 1560, and its Master is an ex officio governor of the school.", "subject": "Westminster School", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Foundation ===\nThe college was founded by Henry VIII in 1546 with the name College of the Holy and Undivided Trinity within the Town and University of Cambridge of King Henry the Eighth's Foundation, from the merger of two existing colleges: Michaelhouse (founded by Hervey de Stanton in 1324), and King's Hall (established by Edward II in 1317 and refounded by Edward III in 1337).", "subject": "Henry VIII", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "At the time, Henry had been seizing (Catholic) church lands from abbeys and monasteries.", "subject": "Henry", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "The universities of Oxford and Cambridge, being both religious institutions and quite rich, expected to be next in line.", "subject": "Oxford", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "The king duly passed an act of Parliament, the Dissolution of Colleges Act 1545 (37 Hen.", "subject": "Colleges Act", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "The universities used their contacts to plead with his sixth wife, Catherine Parr.", "subject": "Catherine Parr", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "The Queen persuaded her husband not to close them down, but to create a new college.", "subject": "The Queen", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "The king did not want to use royal funds, so he instead combined two colleges (King's Hall and Michaelhouse) and seven hostels to form Trinity.", "subject": "Hall", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "=== Nevile's expansion ===\n\nThe monastic lands granted by Henry VIII were not on their own sufficient to ensure Trinity's eventual rise.", "subject": "Henry VIII", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "In terms of architecture and royal association, it was not until the Mastership of Thomas Nevile (1593–1615) that Trinity assumed both its spaciousness and its association with the governing class that distinguished it since the Civil War.", "subject": "Thomas Nevile", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Trinity College, Cambridge", "statement": "In its infancy Trinity had owed a great deal to its neighbouring college of St John's: in the words of Roger Ascham, Trinity was a colonia deducta.", "subject": "St John", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth is conformity to reality or fact.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth is typically treated as a property of truthbearers, such as sentences, propositions, or beliefs that describe things as they are.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Various theories of the nature of truth have been proposed, but its precise definition remains contested.", "subject": "Various", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "According to the coherence theory, truth consists in logical consistency and mutual support among beliefs.", "subject": "According", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Pragmatists understand truth in terms of practical consequences and epistemic practices, claiming that truth is what works or what would withstand the test of unlimited inquiry.", "subject": "Pragmatists", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Deflationary theories argue that truth has no significant intrinsic nature, holding that the linguistic role of truth-related expressions exhausts the concept of truth.", "subject": "Deflationary", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Pluralists assert that the definition of truth varies with the domain of analysis, while relativists maintain that the same statement can be true in one perspective and false in another.", "subject": "Pluralists", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Theories of truth are challenged by logical paradoxes, such as the liar paradox.", "subject": "Theories", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "There are also discussions about the existence of additional truth values besides true and false and about the possibility of truth value gaps—statements that have no truth value.", "subject": "There", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Philosophers distinguish types of truth by domain, content, and epistemic access, including a priori, necessary, moral, aesthetic, religious, and scientific truths.", "subject": "Philosophers", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth plays a central role in the sciences as a goal of inquiry.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Many religious traditions regard truth as a virtue and ground it in a divine source.", "subject": "Many", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth also acts as a guiding norm in the fields of ethics, law, and journalism.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Discussions of truth arose in antiquity in Hindu, Jain, Buddhist, and Greek thought.", "subject": "Discussions", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "In the medieval period, Christian and Islamic philosophers explored the relation between truth and God.", "subject": "Christian", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Discussions in the modern and contemporary periods have addressed diverse topics, such as the nature of truth and its relation to thought, language, and reality.", "subject": "Discussions", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "== Definition ==\nTruth is conformity to facts or accordance with reality.", "subject": "Definition", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "However, its precise definition is disputed, with different theories focusing on elements such as correspondence, coherence, or practical usefulness.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "In a slightly different sense, the term can also refer to genuineness, as in \"a true friend\" or \"true gold\", spiritual teachings, like \"the truth of the scriptures\", or facts themselves, such as \"in truth, the product was defective.\" Truth contrasts with falsehood or falsity, which encompasses misrepresentations that do not meet this standard and fail to align with reality.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth plays a central role in many human endeavors.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "People refer to truth to indicate reliable information, mark scientific findings well supported by evidence, distinguish accurate legal testimony from misrepresentation, and emphasize honesty and sincerity in personal life.", "subject": "People", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "Truth is typically regarded as a positive value, either because of its beneficial consequences or as an intrinsic good pursued for its own sake.", "subject": "Truth", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "The word truth comes from the Old English trēowth, meaning 'fidelity'.", "subject": "Old English", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "It entered Modern English via the Middle English term trewthe.", "subject": "Modern English", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Truth", "statement": "== Basic concepts ==\n\n\n=== Truthbearers ===\n\nTruth is commonly treated as a feature of truthbearers—entities that can be true or false.", "subject": "Basic", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The University of Cambridge is a collegiate public research university in Cambridge, England.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Founded in 1209, the University of Cambridge is the second-oldest university in the English-speaking world and the world's third-oldest university in continuous operation.", "subject": "Founded", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The university's founding followed the arrival of scholars who left the University of Oxford for Cambridge after a dispute with local townspeople.", "subject": "University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The two ancient English universities, although sometimes described as rivals, share many common features and are often jointly referred to as Oxbridge.", "subject": "English", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "In 1231, 22 years after its founding, the university was recognised with a royal charter, granted by King Henry III.", "subject": "King Henry III", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The University of Cambridge includes 31 semi-autonomous constituent colleges and more than 150 academic departments, faculties, and other institutions organised into six schools.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The largest department is Cambridge University Press and Assessment, which contains the oldest university press in the world, with £1 billion of annual revenue and with 100 million learners.", "subject": "Cambridge University Press", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "All of the colleges are self-governing institutions within the university, managing their own personnel and policies, and all students are required to have a college affiliation within the university.", "subject": "All", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Undergraduate teaching at Cambridge is centred on weekly small-group supervisions in the colleges with lectures, seminars, laboratory work, and occasionally further supervision provided by the central university faculties and departments.", "subject": "Undergraduate", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The university operates eight cultural and scientific museums, including the Fitzwilliam Museum and Cambridge University Botanic Garden.", "subject": "Fitzwilliam Museum", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Cambridge's 116 libraries hold a total of approximately 16 million books, around 9 million of which are in Cambridge University Library, a legal deposit library and one of the world's largest academic libraries.", "subject": "Cambridge University Library", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Among the university's notable alumni are reigning King Charles III, Francis Bacon, Lord Byron, Oliver Cromwell, Charles Darwin, Rosalind Franklin, Rajiv Gandhi, John Harvard, Stephen Hawking, John Maynard Keynes, John Milton, Vladimir Nabokov, Jawaharlal Nehru, Isaac Newton, Salman Rushdie, Bertrand Russell, Zadie Smith, Alan Turing, Ludwig Wittgenstein, William Wordsworth, 15 British prime ministers including the sitting Prime Minister Andy Burnham and 194 Olympic medal-winning athletes.", "subject": "King Charles III", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Its alumni, academics, and affiliates have won 126 Nobel Prizes.", "subject": "Nobel Prizes", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Founding ===\n\nPrior to the founding of the University of Cambridge in 1209, Cambridge and the area surrounding it already had developed a scholarly and ecclesiastical reputation largely due to the intellectual and academic contributions of monks from the nearby Ely Cathedral.", "subject": "Ely Cathedral", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The founding of the university, however, mostly originated with an incident at the University of Oxford during which two Oxford scholars, following the death of a local woman, were hanged by the town's secular authorities.", "subject": "University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "During this time, Oxford's town authorities were in conflict with King John.", "subject": "King John", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Fearing more violence from Oxford townsfolk, University of Oxford scholars began leaving Oxford for more hospitable cities, including Paris, Reading, and Cambridge.", "subject": "Fearing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Enough scholars ultimately took residence in Cambridge to form, along with the many scholars already there, the nucleus for the new university's foundation.", "subject": "Enough", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "By 1225, a chancellor of the university was appointed, and writs issued by King Henry III in 1231 established that rents in Cambridge were to be set secundum consuetudinem universitatis, according to the custom of the university, and established a panel of two masters and two townsmen to determine these.", "subject": "King Henry III", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "A letter from Pope Gregory IX two years later to the chancellor and the guild of scholars granted the new university ius non trahi extra, or the right not to be drawn out, for three years, meaning its members could not be summoned to a court outside of the diocese of Ely.", "subject": "Pope Gregory IX", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "After Cambridge was described as a studium generale in a letter from Pope Nicholas IV in 1290, and confirmed as such by Pope John XXII's 1318 papal bull, it became common for researchers from other European medieval universities to visit Cambridge to study or give lectures.", "subject": "After Cambridge", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "=== Foundation of the colleges ===\n\nThe 31 colleges of the present-day University of Cambridge were originally an incidental feature of the university; no college within the University of Cambridge is as old as the university itself.", "subject": "Foundation", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "There were also institutions without endowments, called hostels, which were gradually absorbed by the colleges over the centuries, and they have left some traces, including the naming of Garret Hostel Lane and Garret Hostel Bridge, a street and bridge in Cambridge.", "subject": "Garret Hostel Lane", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "The University of Cambridge's first college, Peterhouse, was founded in 1284 by Hugh de Balsham, the Bishop of Ely.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Cambridge", "statement": "Multiple additional colleges were founded during the 14th and 15th centuries, and colleges continued to be established during modern times, though there was a 204-year gap between the founding of Sidney Sussex in 1596 and that of Downing in 1800.", "subject": "Sidney Sussex", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The University of Göttingen, officially the Georg August University of Göttingen (German: Georg-August-Universität Göttingen, commonly referred to as Georgia Augusta), is a public research university in the city of Göttingen, Lower Saxony, Germany.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Founded in 1734 by George II, King of Great Britain and Elector of Hanover, it began instruction in 1737 and is recognized as the oldest university in Lower Saxony.", "subject": "George II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Recognized for its historic and traditional significance, the university has affiliations with 47 Nobel Prize winners by its own count.", "subject": "Nobel Prize", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The University of Göttingen reached its academic peak from the late 19th to early 20th century, establishing itself as a major international center for mathematics and physics.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "During this period, scholars such as David Hilbert, Felix Klein, Max Born, and Ludwig Prandtl conducted influential research in mathematics, quantum mechanics, and aerodynamics.", "subject": "David Hilbert", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The university attracted international students, including prominent Americans such as Edward Everett, George Bancroft, John Lothrop Motley, and J.", "subject": "Edward Everett", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "This prominence was severely disrupted by the Nazi rise to power in 1933, when the \"great purge\" resulted in the dismissal or emigration of numerous faculty members, including many of Jewish origin or those opposed to the regime.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The university was subsequently reopened under British control in 1945 and began a process of academic reconstruction.", "subject": "British", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Today, the University of Göttingen is a member of the U15 Group of major German research universities.", "subject": "Today,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "It is also a part of prominent international and European academic networks such as The Guild, the ENLIGHT alliance, and the Hekksagon network.", "subject": "The Guild", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The university maintains close collaborations with leading Göttingen-based research institutions such as Max Planck Society, the Leibniz Association, the Fraunhofer Society, and the Helmholtz Association.", "subject": "Max Planck Society", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "With its extensive collection, the Göttingen State and University Library stands among Germany's largest libraries.", "subject": "University Library", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Inauguration ===\nIn 1734, King George II of Great Britain and Elector of Hanover, commanded his Prime Minister in Hanover, Gerlach Adolph von Münchhausen, to establish a university in Göttingen to spread the ideals of the Enlightenment.", "subject": "King George II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Napoleon famously remarked, \"Göttingen belongs neither to a State, nor to Germany, and is the University of Europe\".", "subject": "Napoleon", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The initial university infrastructure was modest, comprising only a riding hall and a fencing house, with lectures held in the Paulinerkirche, Dominican monastery, or professors' homes.", "subject": "Paulinerkirche,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "=== 18th–19th centuries ===\nThroughout the 18th century, the University of Göttingen was renowned among German universities for its commitment to the free spirit and scientific exploration.", "subject": "Throughout", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Georg Christoph Lichtenberg, a prominent scholar, held one of the first professorships dedicated to experimental physics in Germany from 1769 to 1799.", "subject": "Georg Christoph Lichtenberg", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "By 1812, Göttingen had established itself as a modern, internationally recognized university, boasting a library with over 200,000 volumes.", "subject": "Göttingen", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "In the first years of the University of Göttingen, it became known especially for its Faculty of Law.", "subject": "University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "In the 18th century Johann Stephan Pütter, a scholar of public law at that time, taught jus publicum for half a century.", "subject": "Johann Stephan Pütter", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "The subject had attracted students such as Klemens Wenzel Lothar von Metternich, later diplomat and Prime Minister of Austria, and Wilhelm von Humboldt, who later established the University of Berlin.", "subject": "Klemens Wenzel Lothar", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "In the 19th century, Gustav Hugo, Karl Friedrich Eichhorn, and Georg Friedrich Sartorius, who taught law here, became the pioneers of the German Historical School of Jurisprudence.", "subject": "Gustav Hugo", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "At the time, Göttingen was a very popular place for the study of law in Germany: Even the great German poet Heinrich Heine obtained a doctorate in law here in 1825.", "subject": "Heinrich Heine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "Otto von Bismarck, the main creator and the first Chancellor of the second German Empire, also studied law in Göttingen in 1833: he lived in a tiny house on the \"Wall\", now known as \"Bismarck Cottage\".", "subject": "German Empire", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Göttingen", "statement": "According to oral tradition, he lived there because his rowdiness had caused him to be banned from living within the city walls.", "subject": "According", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "The University of Oxford is a collegiate research university in Oxford, England.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "There is evidence of teaching as early as 1096, making it the oldest university in the English-speaking world and the world’s second-oldest university in continuous operation.", "subject": "There", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "It grew rapidly from 1167, when Henry II prohibited English students from attending the University of Paris.", "subject": "Henry II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "After escalating conflict between students and the Oxford townspeople, some Oxford academics fled northeast to Cambridge, where they established the University of Cambridge in 1209.", "subject": "After", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "The two universities share many common features and are jointly referred to as Oxbridge.", "subject": "Oxbridge.", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "The University of Oxford is made up of 43 colleges – of which 36 are chartered colleges (independent bodies), four are permanent private halls (owned by religious organisations), and three are societies (controlled by the university) – and a range of academic departments that are organised into four divisions.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Colleges control their own membership and activities.", "subject": "Colleges", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Typically social life for students is centred around fellow college members.", "subject": "Typically", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "All students are members of a college.", "subject": "All", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Oxford does not have a main campus.", "subject": "Oxford", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Its buildings and facilities are scattered throughout the city centre and around the town.", "subject": "Its", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Undergraduate teaching at the university consists of lectures, small-group tutorials at the colleges and halls, seminars, laboratory work and tutorials provided by the central university faculties and departments.", "subject": "Undergraduate", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Postgraduate teaching is provided in a predominantly centralised fashion.", "subject": "Postgraduate", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Oxford operates the Ashmolean Museum, the world's oldest university museum; Oxford University Press, the largest university press in the world; and the largest academic library system nationwide.", "subject": "Ashmolean Museum", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "In the fiscal year ending 31 July 2025, the university had a total consolidated income of £3.02 billion, of which £801.3 million was from research grants and contracts.", "subject": "July", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Oxford has educated a wide range of notable alumni, including 31 prime ministers of the United Kingdom and many heads of state and government around the world.", "subject": "United Kingdom", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "As of October 2025, 76 Nobel Prize laureates, four Fields Medalists, and six Turing Award winners have matriculated, worked, or held visiting fellowships at the University of Oxford.", "subject": "Nobel Prize", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Its alumni have won 160 Olympic medals.", "subject": "Its", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Oxford is home to a number of scholarships, including the Rhodes Scholarship, one of the oldest international graduate scholarship programmes in the world.", "subject": "Rhodes Scholarship", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Founding ===\n\nThe University of Oxford's foundation date is unknown.", "subject": "The University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "While no official papal bull established Oxford, the university emerged in the 12th century as part of Western Europe's Catholic educational system.", "subject": "Western Europe", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Nevertheless, a scholarly tradition observable in the 16th century claimed a far earlier 7th-century foundation by Archbishop Theodore of Tarsus (668–690).", "subject": "Archbishop Theodore", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "This claim lacks documentary support, at least in the surviving record.", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Early teaching was conducted by clerics like Theobald of Étampes, and the curriculum focused on theology and canon law, reflecting its ecclesiastical roots.", "subject": "Early", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "University of Oxford", "statement": "Most scholars were members of the clergy until the Reformation.", "subject": "Most", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "After Socrates' death, Plato founded the Platonic Academy and Platonic philosophy.", "subject": "Plato", "pid": "P112", "object": "Platonic Academy"}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Western philosophy refers to the philosophical thought, traditions, and works of the Western world.", "subject": "Western", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Historically, the term refers to the philosophical thinking of Western culture, beginning with the ancient Greek philosophy of the pre-Socratics.", "subject": "Historically,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "The word philosophy itself originated from the Ancient Greek φιλοσοφία (philosophía), literally, 'the love of wisdom', from Ancient Greek: φιλεῖν (phileîn), 'to love', and σοφία (sophía), 'wisdom'.", "subject": "Ancient Greek", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Western philosophy stands in contrast to other cultural and regional traditions like Eastern philosophy.", "subject": "Western", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "== History ==\n\n\n=== Ancient ===\n\nThe scope of ancient Western philosophy included the problems of philosophy as they are understood today; but it also included many other disciplines, such as pure mathematics and natural sciences such as physics, astronomy, and biology (Aristotle, for example, wrote on all of these topics).", "subject": "History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "==== Pre-Socratics ====\n\nThe pre-Socratic philosophers were interested in cosmology (the nature and origin of the universe), while rejecting unargued fables in place for argued theory, i.e.", "subject": "Pre-Socratics", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "They were specifically interested in the arche (the cause or first principle) of the world.", "subject": "They", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "The first recognized philosopher, Thales of Miletus (born c.", "subject": "Thales", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "625 BC in Ionia) identified water as the arche (claiming \"all is water\").", "subject": "Ionia)", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "His use of observation and reason to derive this conclusion is the reason for distinguishing him as the first philosopher.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Thales' student Anaximander claimed that the arche was the apeiron, the infinite.", "subject": "Thales'", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Following both Thales and Anaximander, Anaximenes of Miletus claimed that air was the most suitable candidate.", "subject": "Following", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "570 BC), from the island of Samos off the coast of Ionia, later lived in Croton in southern Italy (Magna Graecia).", "subject": "Magna Graecia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Pythagoreans hold that \"all is number\", giving formal accounts in contrast to the previous material of the Ionians.", "subject": "Pythagoreans", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "They also believed in metempsychosis, the transmigration of souls, or reincarnation.", "subject": "They", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Parmenides argued that, unlike the other philosophers who believed the arche was transformed into multiple things, the world must be singular, unchanging and eternal, while anything suggesting the contrary was an illusion.", "subject": "Parmenides", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Zeno of Elea formulated his famous paradoxes in order to support Parmenides' views about the illusion of plurality and change (in terms of motion), by demonstrating them to be impossible.", "subject": "Zeno", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "An alternative explanation was presented by Heraclitus, who claimed that everything was in flux all the time, famously pointing out that one could not step into the same river twice.", "subject": "Heraclitus,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Empedocles may have been an associate of both Parmenides and the Pythagoreans.", "subject": "Empedocles", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "These in turn were acted upon by the forces of Love and Strife, creating the mixtures of elements which form the world.", "subject": "These", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Another view of the arche being acted upon by an external force was presented by his older contemporary Anaxagoras, who claimed that nous, the mind, was responsible for that.", "subject": "Another", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Leucippus and Democritus proposed atomism as an explanation for the fundamental nature of the universe.", "subject": "Leucippus", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "Jonathan Barnes called atomism \"the culmination of early Greek thought\".", "subject": "Jonathan Barnes", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Western philosophy", "statement": "In addition to these philosophers, the Sophists comprised teachers of rhetoric who taught students to debate on any side of an issue.", "subject": "Sophists", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "The company was founded in 1807 and produces books, journals, and encyclopedias, in print and electronically, as well as online products and services, training materials, and educational materials for undergraduate, graduate, and continuing education students.", "subject": "The company", "pid": "P571", "object": "1807"}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "John Wiley & Sons, Inc., commonly known as Wiley (), is an American multinational publishing company which focuses on academic publishing and instructional materials.", "subject": "John Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "== History ==\n\nThe company was established in 1807 when Charles Wiley opened a print shop in Manhattan.", "subject": "Charles Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "The company was the publisher of 19th century American literary figures like James Fenimore Cooper, Washington Irving, Herman Melville, and Edgar Allan Poe, as well as of legal, religious, and other non-fiction titles.", "subject": "James Fenimore Cooper", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley later shifted its focus to scientific, technical, and engineering subject areas, abandoning its literary interests.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley's son John (born in Flatbush in Brooklyn, New York City, on October 4, 1808; died in East Orange, New Jersey, on February 21, 1891) took over the business when Charles Wiley died in 1826.", "subject": "New York City", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "The firm was successively named Wiley, Lane & Co., next Wiley & Putnam, and then John Wiley.", "subject": "John Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "The company acquired its present name in 1876, when John's second son William H.", "subject": "John's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley joined his brother Charles in the business.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Through the 20th century, the company expanded its publishing activities, the sciences, and higher education.", "subject": "Through", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1960 Wiley set up a European branch in London, which later moved to Chichester, England.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1982, Wiley acquired the publishing operations of the British firm Heyden & Son.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1989, Wiley acquired the life science publisher Liss.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1996, Wiley acquired the German technical publisher VCH.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1997, Wiley acquired the professional publisher Van Nostrand Reinhold (the successor to the company started by David Van Nostrand) from Thomson Learning.", "subject": "Van Nostrand Reinhold", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 1999, Wiley acquired the professional publisher Jossey-Bass from Pearson.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 2001, Wiley acquired the publisher Hungry Minds (formerly IDG Books, including most titles formerly published by Macmillan General Reference) from International Data Group.", "subject": "Hungry Minds", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 2005, Wiley acquired the British medical publisher Whurr.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley marked its bicentennial in 2007.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In conjunction with the anniversary, the company published Knowledge for Generations: Wiley and the Global Publishing Industry, 1807–2007, depicting Wiley's role in the evolution of publishing against a social, cultural, and economic backdrop.", "subject": "Global Publishing Industry", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley has also created an online community called Wiley Living History, offering excerpts from Knowledge for Generations and a forum for visitors and Wiley employees to post their comments and anecdotes.", "subject": "Wiley Living History", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 2021, Wiley acquired Hindawi and J&J Editorial.", "subject": "Wiley", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "In 2023, Academic Partnerships acquired Wiley's online education business for $150 million.", "subject": "Academic Partnerships", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "=== Emerging markets ===\nIn December 2010, Wiley opened an office in Dubai.", "subject": "In December", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Wiley (publisher)", "statement": "Wiley established publishing operations in India in 2006 (though it has had a sales presence since 1966), and has established a presence in North Africa through sales contracts with academic institutions in Tunisia, Libya, and Egypt.", "subject": "North Africa", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Willard Van Orman Quine ( KWYNE; known to his friends as \"Van\"; June 25, 1908 – December 25, 2000) was an American logician and philosopher in the analytic tradition, recognized as \"one of the most influential philosophers of the twentieth century\".", "subject": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "He was the Edgar Pierce Chair of Philosophy at Harvard University from 1956 to 1978.", "subject": "Edgar Pierce Chair", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Quine was a teacher of logic and set theory.", "subject": "Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "He was famous for his position that first-order logic is the only kind worthy of the name, and developed his own system of mathematics and set theory, known as New Foundations.", "subject": "New Foundations", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "In the philosophy of mathematics, he and his Harvard colleague Hilary Putnam developed the Quine–Putnam indispensability argument, an argument for the reality of mathematical entities.", "subject": "Hilary Putnam", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "This led to his famous quip that \"philosophy of science is philosophy enough\".", "subject": "This", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "He also advocated holism in science, known as the Duhem–Quine thesis.", "subject": "Duhem–Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "His major writings include the papers \"On What There Is\" (1948), which elucidated Bertrand Russell's theory of descriptions and contains Quine's famous dictum of ontological commitment, \"To be is to be the value of a variable\", and \"Two Dogmas of Empiricism\" (1951), which attacked the traditional analytic-synthetic distinction and reductionism, undermining the then-popular logical positivism, advocating instead a form of semantic holism and ontological relativity.", "subject": "On What There Is", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "They also include the books The Web of Belief (1970), which advocates a kind of coherentism, and Word and Object (1960), which further developed these positions and introduced Quine's famous indeterminacy of translation thesis, advocating a behaviorist theory of meaning.", "subject": "The Web", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "== Biography ==\nQuine's parents were Cloyd Robert Quine and Harriet Ellis Van Orman.", "subject": "Cloyd Robert Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Quine grew up in Akron, Ohio, where he lived with his parents and older brother Robert Cloyd.", "subject": "Robert Cloyd", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "His father was a manufacturing entrepreneur (founder of the Akron Equipment Company, which produced tire molds) and his mother was a schoolteacher and housewife.", "subject": "Akron Equipment Company", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Quine became an atheist around the age of 9 and remained one for the rest of his life.", "subject": "Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "=== Education ===\nQuine received his B.A., summa cum laude, in mathematics from Oberlin College in 1930, and his Ph.D.", "subject": "Oberlin College", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "in philosophy from Harvard University in 1932.", "subject": "Harvard University", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "His thesis supervisor was Alfred North Whitehead.", "subject": "Alfred North Whitehead", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "He was then appointed a Harvard Junior Fellow, which excused him from having to teach for four years.", "subject": "Harvard Junior Fellow", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "During the academic year 1932–33, he travelled in Europe thanks to a Sheldon Fellowship, meeting Polish logicians (including Jan Łukasiewicz, Stanislaw Lesniewski and Alfred Tarski) and members of the Vienna Circle (including Rudolf Carnap), as well as the logical positivist A.", "subject": "Sheldon Fellowship", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "It was in Prague that Quine developed a passion for philosophy, thanks to Carnap, whom he called his \"true and only maître à penser\".", "subject": "Prague", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "=== World War II ===\nQuine arranged for Tarski to be invited to the September 1939 Unity of Science Congress in Cambridge, for which the Jewish Tarski sailed on the last ship to leave Danzig before Nazi Germany invaded Poland and triggered World War II.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Tarski survived the war and worked another 44 years in the US.", "subject": "Tarski", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "During the war, Quine lectured on logic in Brazil, in Portuguese, and served in the United States Navy in a military intelligence role, deciphering messages from German submarines, and reaching the rank of lieutenant commander.", "subject": "United States Navy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Quine could lecture in French, German, Italian, Portuguese, and Spanish as well as his native English.", "subject": "Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "=== Personal ===\nHe had four children by two marriages.", "subject": "Personal", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Willard Van Orman Quine", "statement": "Guitarist Robert Quine was his nephew.", "subject": "Guitarist Robert Quine", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "The company was founded in 1981.", "subject": "The company", "pid": "P571", "object": "1981"}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "World Scientific Publishing is an academic publisher of scientific, technical, and medical books and journals headquartered in Singapore.", "subject": "World Scientific Publishing", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "In 1995, World Scientific co-founded the London-based Imperial College Press together with the Imperial College of Science, Technology and Medicine.", "subject": "World Scientific", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "== Company structure ==\nThe company head office is in Singapore.", "subject": "Company", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "The Chairman and Editor-in-Chief is Dr Phua Kok Khoo, while the Managing Director is Doreen Liu.", "subject": "The Chairman", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "=== Imperial College Press ===\nIn 1995 the company co-founded Imperial College Press, specializing in engineering, medicine and information technology, with Imperial College London.", "subject": "Imperial College Press", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "In 2006, World Scientific assumed full ownership of Imperial College Press, under a license granted by the university.", "subject": "World Scientific", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "Finally, in August 2016, ICP was fully incorporated into World Scientific under the new imprint, World Scientific Europe.", "subject": "World Scientific", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "== See also ==\n\nJournals published by World Scientific\n\n\n== References ==\n\n\n== External links ==\nOfficial website", "subject": "World Scientific", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "The company was co-founded by them in 1981.", "subject": "The", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "It publishes about 600 books annually, with more than 170 journals in various fields.", "subject": "Unknown", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "Company structure ==", "subject": "Company", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "The company head office is in Singapore.", "subject": "The", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "Imperial College Press ===", "subject": "Imperial College Press", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World Scientific", "statement": "In 1995 the company co-founded Imperial College Press, specializing in engineering, medicine and information technology, with Imperial College London.", "subject": "Imperial College Press", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "World War II, or the Second World War (1 September 1939 – 2 September 1945), was a global conflict between two coalitions: the Allies and the Axis powers.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Nearly all of the world's countries participated, with many engaging in total war on an unprecedented scale.", "subject": "Nearly", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "World War II was the deadliest conflict in history, causing the deaths of 60 to 75 million people, a majority of whom were civilians.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Millions died as a result of massacres, starvation, disease, and genocides including the Holocaust.", "subject": "Millions", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "After the Allied victory, Germany, Austria, Japan, and Korea were occupied, and German and Japanese leaders were tried for war crimes.", "subject": "After", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "The causes of World War II included unresolved tensions in the aftermath of World War I and the rise of fascism in Europe and militarism in Japan.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Key events preceding the war included Japan's invasion of Manchuria in 1931, the Spanish Civil War, the outbreak of the Second Sino-Japanese War in 1937, and Germany's annexations of Austria and the Sudetenland.", "subject": "Spanish Civil War", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "World War II is generally considered to have begun on 1 September 1939, when Nazi Germany, under Adolf Hitler, invaded Poland, after which the United Kingdom and France declared war on Germany.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Poland was also invaded by the Soviet Union in mid-September, and was partitioned between the two states under the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact.", "subject": "Soviet Union", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "In 1940, the Soviet Union annexed the Baltic states and parts of Finland and Romania, while Germany conquered Norway, Denmark, Belgium, Luxembourg, and the Netherlands.", "subject": "Soviet Union", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "After the fall of France in June 1940, the war continued, mainly between Germany, now assisted by Fascist Italy, and the British Empire and British Commonwealth, with fighting in the Balkans, Mediterranean, Middle East, East Africa, the aerial Battle of Britain, the Blitz, and the naval Battle of the Atlantic.", "subject": "Fascist Italy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "By mid-1941, Yugoslavia and Greece had also been defeated by Axis countries.", "subject": "Yugoslavia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "In June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union, opening the Eastern Front.", "subject": "In June", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "In December 1941, Japan attacked American and British territories in Asia and the Pacific, including Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, leading the United States to enter the war against the Axis.", "subject": "In December", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Japan conquered much of coastal China and Southeast Asia, but its advances in the Pacific were halted in June 1942 at the Battle of Midway.", "subject": "Southeast Asia", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "In early 1943, Axis forces were defeated in North Africa and at Stalingrad in the Soviet Union.", "subject": "North Africa", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "An Allied invasion of Italy in July resulted in the fall of its fascist regime, and Allied offensives in the Pacific and the Soviet Union forced the Axis to retreat on all fronts.", "subject": "An Allied", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "In 1944, the Western Allies invaded France at Normandy, opening a new front, and the Soviet Union advanced into Central Europe.", "subject": "Western Allies", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Japan also suffered major setbacks, including the crippling of its navy by the United States, the loss of key Western Pacific islands, and defeats in Burma.", "subject": "United States", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "The war in Europe concluded with the liberation of German-occupied territories and an invasion of Germany itself, which culminated in the fall of Berlin and Germany's unconditional surrender on 8 May 1945.", "subject": "Europe", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "On 6 and 9 August, the US dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, followed by a Soviet invasion of Japanese-occupied Manchuria.", "subject": "August,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "Japan announced its unconditional surrender on 15 August and signed a surrender document on 2 September 1945.", "subject": "Japan", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "World War II transformed the political, economic, and social structures of the world and established the foundation of international relations for the rest of the 20th century and into the 21st century.", "subject": "World War II", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "The United Nations was created to foster international cooperation and prevent future conflicts, with the victorious great powers—China, France, the Soviet Union, the United Kingdom, and the United States—becoming the permanent members of its Security Council.", "subject": "The United Nations", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "World War II", "statement": "The Soviet Union and the United States emerged as rival superpowers, setting the stage for the Cold War.", "subject": "The Soviet Union", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "In set theory, Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory, named after mathematicians Ernst Zermelo and Abraham Fraenkel, is an axiomatic system that was proposed in the early twentieth century in order to formulate a theory of sets free of paradoxes such as Russell's paradox.", "subject": "Ernst Zermelo", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Today, Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory, with the historically controversial axiom of choice (AC) included, is the standard form of axiomatic set theory and as such is the most common foundation of mathematics.", "subject": "Today,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice included is abbreviated ZFC, where C stands for \"choice\", and ZF refers to the axioms of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice excluded.", "subject": "Zermelo–Fraenkel", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Informally, Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory is intended to formalize a single primitive notion, that of a hereditary well-founded set, so that all entities in the universe of discourse are such sets.", "subject": "Informally,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Thus the axioms of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory refer only to pure sets and prevent its models from containing urelements (elements that are not themselves sets).", "subject": "Thus", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Furthermore, proper classes (collections of mathematical objects defined by a property shared by their members where the collections are too big to be sets) can only be treated indirectly.", "subject": "Furthermore,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Specifically, Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory does not allow for the existence of a universal set (a set containing all sets) nor for unrestricted comprehension, thereby avoiding Russell's paradox.", "subject": "Specifically,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Von Neumann–Bernays–Gödel set theory (NBG) is a commonly used conservative extension of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory that does allow explicit treatment of proper classes.", "subject": "Von Neumann", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "There are many equivalent formulations of the axioms of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory.", "subject": "There", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Most of the axioms state the existence of particular sets defined from other sets.", "subject": "Most", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "For example, the axiom of pairing implies  that given any two sets \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n  \n and \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n  \n there is a new set \n  \n    \n      \n        {\n        a\n        ,\n        b\n        }\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\{a,b\\}}\n  \n containing exactly \n  \n    \n      \n        a\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle a}\n  \n and \n  \n    \n      \n        b\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle b}\n  \n.", "subject": "For", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Other axioms describe properties of set membership.", "subject": "Other", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "A goal of the axioms is that each axiom should be true if interpreted as a statement about the collection of all sets in the von Neumann universe (also known as the cumulative hierarchy).", "subject": "Neumann", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "The metamathematics of Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory has been extensively studied.", "subject": "Zermelo–Fraenkel", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Landmark results in this area established the logical independence of the axiom of choice from the remaining Zermelo-Fraenkel axioms and of the continuum hypothesis from ZFC.", "subject": "Landmark", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "The consistency of a theory such as ZFC cannot be proved within the theory itself, as shown by Gödel's second incompleteness theorem.", "subject": "ZFC", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "== History ==\n\nThe modern study of set theory was initiated by Georg Cantor and Richard Dedekind in the 1870s.", "subject": "Georg Cantor", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "However, the discovery of paradoxes in naive set theory, such as Russell's paradox, led to the desire for a more rigorous form of set theory that was free of these paradoxes.", "subject": "However,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "In 1908, Ernst Zermelo proposed the first axiomatic set theory, Zermelo set theory.", "subject": "Ernst Zermelo", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "However, as first pointed out by Abraham Fraenkel in a 1921 letter to Zermelo, this theory was incapable of proving the existence of certain sets and cardinal numbers whose existence was taken for granted by most set theorists of the time, notably the cardinal number aleph-omega (\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          ℵ\n          \n            ω\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\aleph _{\\omega }}\n  \n) and the set \n  \n    \n      \n        {\n        \n          Z\n          \n            0\n          \n        \n        ,\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          Z\n          \n            0\n          \n        \n        )\n        ,\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          Z\n          \n            0\n          \n        \n        )\n        )\n        ,\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n        (\n        \n          Z\n          \n            0\n          \n        \n        )\n        )\n        )\n        ,\n        .", "subject": "Abraham Fraenkel", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "}\n        ,\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\{Z_{0},{\\mathcal {P}}(Z_{0}),{\\mathcal {P}}({\\mathcal {P}}(Z_{0})),{\\mathcal {P}}({\\mathcal {P}}({\\mathcal {P}}(Z_{0}))),...\\},}\n  \n where \n  \n    \n      \n        \n          Z\n          \n            0\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle Z_{0}}\n  \n is any infinite set and \n  \n    \n      \n        \n          \n            P\n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle {\\mathcal {P}}}\n  \n is the power set operation.", "subject": "Z_{0}}", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Moreover, one of Zermelo's axioms invoked a concept, that of a \"definite\" property, whose operational meaning was not clear.", "subject": "Moreover,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "In 1922, Fraenkel and Thoralf Skolem independently proposed operationalizing a \"definite\" property as one that could be formulated as a well-formed formula in a first-order logic whose atomic formulas were limited to set membership and identity.", "subject": "Thoralf Skolem", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "They also independently proposed replacing the axiom schema of specification with the axiom schema of replacement.", "subject": "They", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory", "statement": "Appending this schema, as well as the axiom of regularity (first proposed by John von Neumann), to Zermelo set theory yields the theory ZFC.", "subject": "Appending", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Évariste Galois (; French: [evaʁist ɡalwa]; 25 October 1811 – 31 May 1832) was a French mathematician and political activist.", "subject": "Évariste", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "While still in his teens, he was able to determine a necessary and sufficient condition for a polynomial to be solvable by radicals, thereby solving a problem that had been open for 350 years.", "subject": "While", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "His work laid the foundations for Galois theory and group theory, two major branches of abstract algebra.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Galois was a staunch Republican and was heavily involved in the political turmoil that surrounded the French Revolution of 1830.", "subject": "French Revolution", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "For reasons that remain obscure, shortly after his release from prison, Galois fought in a duel and died of the wounds he suffered.", "subject": "For", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "== Life ==\n\n\n=== Early life ===\nGalois was born on 25 October 1811 to Nicolas-Gabriel Galois and Adélaïde-Marie (née Demante).", "subject": "Gabriel Galois", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "His father was a Republican and was head of Bourg-la-Reine's liberal party.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "His father became mayor of the village after Louis XVIII returned to the throne in 1814.", "subject": "Louis XVIII", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "His mother, the daughter of a jurist, was a fluent reader of Latin and classical literature and was responsible for her son's education for his first twelve years.", "subject": "His", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "In October 1823, he entered the Lycée Louis-le-Grand where his teacher Louis Paul Émile Richard recognized his brilliance.", "subject": "In October", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Galois found a copy of Adrien-Marie Legendre's Éléments de Géométrie, which, it is said, he read \"like a novel\" and mastered at the first reading.", "subject": "Marie Legendre", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "At 15, he was reading the original papers of Joseph-Louis Lagrange, such as the Réflexions sur la résolution algébrique des équations which likely motivated his later work on equation theory, and Leçons sur le calcul des fonctions, work intended for professional mathematicians, yet his classwork remained uninspired and his teachers accused him of putting on the airs of a genius.", "subject": "Louis Lagrange", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "=== Budding mathematician ===\nIn 1828, Galois attempted the entrance examination for the École Polytechnique, the most prestigious institution for mathematics in France at the time, without the usual preparation in mathematics, and failed for lack of explanations on the oral examination.", "subject": "Budding", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "In that same year, he entered the École Normale (then known as l'École préparatoire), a far inferior institution for mathematical studies at that time, where he found some professors sympathetic to him.", "subject": "École", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "In the following year Galois's first paper, on simple continued fractions, \nwas published.", "subject": "Galois's", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "He submitted two papers on this topic to the Academy of Sciences.", "subject": "Academy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Augustin-Louis Cauchy refereed these papers, but refused to accept them for publication for reasons that still remain unclear.", "subject": "Louis Cauchy", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "However, in spite of many claims to the contrary, it is widely held that Cauchy recognized the importance of Galois's work, and that he merely suggested combining the two papers into one in order to enter it in the competition for the academy's Grand Prize in Mathematics.", "subject": "Grand Prize", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Cauchy, an eminent mathematician of the time though with political views that were diametrically opposed to those of Galois, considered Galois's work to be a likely winner.", "subject": "Cauchy,", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "On 28 July 1829, Galois's father died by suicide after a bitter political dispute with the village priest.", "subject": "July", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "A couple of days later, Galois made his second and last attempt to enter the Polytechnique and failed yet again.", "subject": "Galois", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "It is undisputed that Galois was more than qualified; accounts differ on why he failed.", "subject": "Galois", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "More plausible accounts state that Galois made too many logical leaps and baffled the incompetent examiner, which enraged Galois.", "subject": "More", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "Having been denied admission to the École polytechnique, Galois took the Baccalaureate examinations in order to enter the École normale.", "subject": "Having", "pid": null, "object": null}
{"page": "Évariste Galois", "statement": "He passed, receiving his degree on 29 December 1829.", "subject": "December", "pid": null, "object": null}
